module gunbc.live_deploy.deployed_tree_scope


// WHAT THE DEPLOY ACTUALLY INSTALLS, DECLARED ONCE BECAUSE TWO THINGS NEED IT AND THEY MUST NOT DISAGREE.
// The working-tree leg of the source sync rsyncs the runner checkout wholesale except these prefixes, so
// the deployed source is the WORKING TREE, not the commit. Two consumers read that fact:
//
//   gunbc.live_deploy.emit       renders `--exclude /<p>` per prefix into the tree-sync ExecStart
//   gunbc.live_deploy.candidate  decides whether an untracked path would actually be shipped
//
// Spelling it in both would let them drift, and the drift is the dangerous direction: admission would pass
// on a path the sync then installs. Same fact, one home (DESIGN §3).
//
// IT LIVES IN ITS OWN LEAF MODULE FOR A STRUCTURAL REASON, not for tidiness. Putting it beside the emitter
// and importing that from candidate closes a real cycle — candidate is reached from gunbc.hostname_read,
// which gunbc.host_effect reaches, which the emitter's own closure reaches — and acyclicity is the import
// graph's one structural law (DESIGN §3). A fact needed by two modules on opposite sides of a dependency
// chain belongs beneath both, not inside either.
// WHY THIS LIST IS NOT JUST target AND .git, AND WHY IT IS STILL NOT EVERY IGNORED PATH.
// The working-tree leg is rsync, which does not read .gitignore, so an ignored path outside these prefixes
// is copied to the host under a revision that does not name it. Candidate admission refuses on exactly that
// state (gunbc.live_deploy.candidate, the ignored_deployed arm), and the refusal names two remedies: widen
// these exclusions, or remove the file. The four appended here take the first remedy because they are host
// state by construction -- agent session state and local tool/build caches that the deployed tree must never
// contain under any revision, so shipping one is never the intent and refusing on one is pure friction.
//
// THE .env FAMILY IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT AND STAYS REFUSED. The repository ignores `.env`, `.env.local` and
// `.env.*.local`; the third is a wildcard, and both this predicate and rsync's `--exclude /<p>` match a
// literal segment. Listing the two spellable forms would silence admission on them while `.env.*.local` kept
// shipping -- partial cover on the one class where the shipped bytes are secrets, which is strictly worse
// than the present loud refusal on all three. It stays refused until the exclusion carrier can express a
// pattern, and the same reasoning holds for src/v2/test/claim/manual/sg2_scratch_probe.dag for a different
// reason: it sits inside a source root the graph actually reads, so excluding it would change what the host
// compiles rather than merely what it stores.
//
// NOT DERIVED FROM gunbc.gitignore_authority, ON PURPOSE. That authority answers "must not be tracked"; this
// one answers "must not be shipped", and the sets genuinely differ -- a tracked file ships and is not
// ignored. Deriving would also let an ordinary .gitignore edit silently change what a deploy installs, and
// would put an import edge into a module kept leaf for the acyclicity reason stated above.
data deployed_tree_excluded_prefixes: List<String> = [
  "target", ".git", ".claude", ".gunbc", "target-codex", ".cargo-home",
]

fn deployed_tree_rsync_exclude_flags() -> String {
  join(map(deployed_tree_excluded_prefixes, p => concat("--exclude /", p)), " ")
}

// Segment-wise, never a character run: `targetish/x` starts with `target` but is not under it, and treating
// it as excluded would hide from admission a path rsync goes on to ship.
fn path_is_deployed(path: String) -> Bool {
  !any(deployed_tree_excluded_prefixes, p => path == p || starts_with(s: path, prefix: concat(p, "/")))
}
